/-!
# The maximum of an array

The Rust function computes the largest element of an array of bytes with a
`while` loop:

```rust
pub fn max_element<const N: usize>(data: [u8; N]) -> u8 {
    let mut max: u8 = data[0];
    let mut i: usize = 1;
    while i < N {
        if data[i] > max {
            max = data[i];
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    max
}
```

The length `N` is a parameter of the function, a const generic, and it is the
length in the array type `[u8; N]`. For `N = 0`, the access `data[0]` is outside
the array, and the function panics at run time; since `N` is a parameter, the
compiler cannot reject the access.

## The generated definitions

* The type `Array Std.U8 N`, with `N : Std.Usize`, is the Lean model of
  `[u8; N]`. A term of it is a list of bytes together with a proof that its
  length is `N.val`.
* The access `data[i]` becomes `Array.index_usize data i`, which fails when
  `i` is not less than `N`.
* The loop becomes two definitions. The function `max_element_loop.body`
  performs one iteration. It takes the array `data`, which the loop does not
  modify, and the current values `max` and `i` of the two mutable variables.
  It returns `ok (cont (max1, i2))` to continue with new values, or
  `ok (done max)` to leave the loop with a result; the type `ControlFlow` has
  these two constructors. In `ControlFlow (Std.U8 × Std.Usize) Std.U8`, the first
  argument is the type of the values carried by `cont` and the second the type
  of the result carried by `done`. This is the type `ControlFlow` of Aeneas,
  not its model of the Rust type `core::ops::ControlFlow`, whose constructors
  are `Continue` and `Break`.
* The pair of the current values is the **state** of the loop. The function
  `max_element_loop` turns the body into a function of the state and applies
  the library function `loop` to it. The term `loop body x` evaluates `body x`;
  on `cont x'` it continues with `loop body x'`, on `done y` it returns `ok y`,
  and it fails when `body x` fails. The function `loop` is defined with
  `partial_fixpoint`, which does not require a termination proof, since a Rust
  loop need not terminate: a loop that runs forever has the value `div`. Since
  `div` never satisfies a specification `⦃ ⦄`, proving a specification of a
  loop also proves that the loop terminates.
* Aeneas names the values it introduces by counting, so the same name can mean
  different things. In the body, `i1` is the byte read from the array and the
  index is `i`; in `max_element_loop`, `i1` is the index.
-/

namespace max_array

/-- [max_array::max_element]: loop body 0:
    Source: 'max_array/src/lib.rs', lines 4:4-9:5
    Visibility: public -/
@[rust_loop_body]
def max_element_loop.body
  {N : Std.Usize} (data : Array Std.U8 N) (max : Std.U8) (i : Std.Usize) :
  Result (ControlFlow (Std.U8 × Std.Usize) Std.U8)
  := do
  if i < N
  then
    let i1 ← Array.index_usize data i
    let max1 ← if i1 > max
                 then ok i1
                 else ok max
    let i2 ← i + 1#usize
    ok (cont (max1, i2))
  else ok (done max)

/-- [max_array::max_element]: loop 0:
    Source: 'max_array/src/lib.rs', lines 4:4-9:5
    Visibility: public -/
@[rust_loop]
def max_element_loop
  {N : Std.Usize} (data : Array Std.U8 N) (max : Std.U8) (i : Std.Usize) :
  Result Std.U8
  := do
  loop
    (fun (max1, i1) => max_element_loop.body data max1 i1)
    (max, i)

/-- [max_array::max_element]:
    Source: 'max_array/src/lib.rs', lines 1:0-11:1
    Visibility: public -/
def max_element {N : Std.Usize} (data : Array Std.U8 N) : Result Std.U8 := do
  let max ← Array.index_usize data 0#usize
  max_element_loop data max 1#usize


